1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:05,000
1890, a magical time.

2
00:00:05,001 --> 00:00:10,000
The reason? From this date on, motion can be captured on celluloid.

3
00:00:10,001 --> 00:00:14,000
Sports, disappearing cultures, provocative entertainment,

4
00:00:14,001 --> 00:00:17,000
and there's money to be made. Lots of it.

5
00:00:17,001 --> 00:00:22,000
Thomas Edison holds many of the patents, but did he really invent motion pictures?

6
00:00:22,001 --> 00:00:28,000
Now the real story behind the birth of the movies on Modern Marvels.

7
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:35,500
This is the United States.

8
00:00:35,600 --> 00:00:37,500
This is the United States.

9
00:00:37,600 --> 00:00:39,740
This is the United States.

10
00:00:39,840 --> 00:00:43,440
1928, Thomas Alva Edison receives

11
00:00:43,540 --> 00:00:46,180
the Congressional Gold Medal, the nation's highest

12
00:00:46,280 --> 00:00:47,520
civilian honor.

13
00:00:47,620 --> 00:00:51,220
At 81, he's a living legend.

14
00:00:51,320 --> 00:00:54,660
The man who'd laid the technological foundations

15
00:00:54,760 --> 00:00:57,760
for the 20th century, and created motion pictures

16
00:00:57,761 --> 00:01:00,760
for posterity, along with his own accomplishments.

17
00:01:03,660 --> 00:01:06,760
The first motion picture studio ever set up in the world

18
00:01:06,860 --> 00:01:10,300
was in the yard of my laboratory at West Orange.

19
00:01:10,400 --> 00:01:13,100
The building evolved to follow the sun.

20
00:01:13,200 --> 00:01:16,220
We dubbed it the Black Maria.

21
00:01:16,320 --> 00:01:20,220
As recreated with his own invention, the motion picture,

22
00:01:20,320 --> 00:01:23,920
Edison was portrayed as a mix of Horatio Alger pluck,

23
00:01:24,020 --> 00:01:27,520
homespun Yankee ingenuity, and visionary

24
00:01:27,521 --> 00:01:32,520
These are scenes from a 1915 epic entitled The Benefactor.

25
00:01:32,620 --> 00:01:35,020
From the beginning, his life had the makings

26
00:01:35,120 --> 00:01:37,060
of a great movie.

27
00:01:38,960 --> 00:01:41,760
In a sense, Edison devised his own legend

28
00:01:41,860 --> 00:01:44,740
of this sort of rough-hewn inventor

29
00:01:44,840 --> 00:01:47,580
who didn't pay much attention to science.

30
00:01:47,680 --> 00:01:49,580
In fact, Edison paid a lot of attention to science.

31
00:01:49,680 --> 00:01:53,020
What he didn't pay attention to was the kind of higher level,

32
00:01:53,120 --> 00:01:56,020
pure science that didn't have immediate knowledge

33
00:01:56,021 --> 00:01:58,820
that didn't have a media practical application.

34
00:01:58,920 --> 00:02:02,600
30 years old, Edison applied his practical genius

35
00:02:02,700 --> 00:02:05,400
and produced the talking machine.

36
00:02:05,500 --> 00:02:08,900
Topping himself two years later, he lit up the world

37
00:02:09,000 --> 00:02:10,400
with the electric light.

38
00:02:13,800 --> 00:02:17,740
By then, there were people who believed he could do anything,

39
00:02:17,840 --> 00:02:21,680
most of all, Edison himself.

40
00:02:21,780 --> 00:02:24,780
Most inventors have rather large egos.

41
00:02:24,781 --> 00:02:29,123
egos, they feel they can affect the way other people live their lives.

42
00:02:29,124 --> 00:02:30,860
And the way they can do that

43
00:02:30,861 --> 00:02:36,780
is not through management or necessarily through ideas, intellectual ideas, but rather through

44
00:02:36,781 --> 00:02:42,393
creating technologies and systems that people will live with and work with.

45
00:02:42,394 --> 00:02:44,040
Edison's self-absorbed

46
00:02:44,041 --> 00:02:48,842
involvement with his work had disastrous effects on a first marriage.

47
00:02:48,843 --> 00:02:50,860
Weakened by declining health,

48
00:02:50,861 --> 00:02:55,340
his wife Mary Stillwell died in 1884, neglected,

49
00:02:55,440 --> 00:02:56,840
and mentally unstable.

50
00:02:59,740 --> 00:03:05,600
Left with three children, Edison remarried in 1887.

51
00:03:05,700 --> 00:03:11,380
His second wife, Mina Miller, was the daughter of a millionaire.

52
00:03:11,480 --> 00:03:15,460
Edison remained Edison, but with the support of family and friends,

53
00:03:15,560 --> 00:03:19,160
Mina was better prepared to be the wife of a man called

54
00:03:19,161 --> 00:03:23,200
the Wizard of Menlo Park.

55
00:03:23,300 --> 00:03:27,160
Bursting with ideas for new inventions, in 1887,

56
00:03:27,260 --> 00:03:30,400
Edison moved into a huge new laboratory complex

57
00:03:30,500 --> 00:03:34,840
in West Orange, New Jersey, 10 times larger

58
00:03:34,940 --> 00:03:36,820
than his old quarters a few miles away

59
00:03:36,920 --> 00:03:39,580
in the village of Menlo Park.

60
00:03:39,680 --> 00:03:42,820
For Mina and his children, he bought Glenmont,

61
00:03:42,920 --> 00:03:44,620
a lavish nearby estate.

62
00:03:44,621 --> 00:03:49,920
Settled in West Orange, it wasn't long before Edison

63
00:03:50,020 --> 00:03:52,260
found himself at his desk in his library,

64
00:03:52,360 --> 00:03:56,660
thinking about the realization of a very old dream,

65
00:03:56,760 --> 00:03:57,860
moving pictures.

66
00:04:01,200 --> 00:04:06,580
This book, written in 1646 by Affimatius Kircher, a Jesuit

67
00:04:06,680 --> 00:04:12,020
priest, records the early history of projected images.

68
00:04:12,021 --> 00:04:14,620
It began with the magic lantern, forerunner

69
00:04:14,720 --> 00:04:18,220
of today's slide projector.

70
00:04:18,320 --> 00:04:20,420
The magic lantern was a device that

71
00:04:20,520 --> 00:04:24,560
used light from a lamp to project images painted on glass

72
00:04:24,660 --> 00:04:26,760
plates.

73
00:04:26,860 --> 00:04:29,680
The effect on viewers was truly magical,

74
00:04:29,780 --> 00:04:33,680
and these were only static drawings.

75
00:04:33,780 --> 00:04:36,640
Eager for the energy of motion, early inventors

76
00:04:36,740 --> 00:04:42,020
created ingenious devices to add life to their shows.

77
00:04:42,021 --> 00:04:46,160
Slides with movable parts were popular.

78
00:04:46,260 --> 00:04:48,660
A more sophisticated illusion of movement

79
00:04:48,760 --> 00:04:53,400
came in 1834 with the creation of the zoetrope

80
00:04:53,500 --> 00:04:56,840
by English inventor George Homer.

81
00:04:56,940 --> 00:05:01,700
Here, spinning sequential images appear to move.

82
00:05:01,800 --> 00:05:04,540
It was an example of a physiological phenomenon

83
00:05:04,640 --> 00:05:09,540
described in 1884 by Peter Marc Roget of thesaurus fame.

84
00:05:12,020 --> 00:05:15,460
It's called persistence of vision.

85
00:05:15,560 --> 00:05:18,260
With cinema, when you project an image on the screen,

86
00:05:18,360 --> 00:05:20,260
which creates the illusion of motion,

87
00:05:20,360 --> 00:05:22,060
what you're projecting on the screen

88
00:05:22,160 --> 00:05:24,560
is really a series of still photographs.

89
00:05:24,660 --> 00:05:27,100
And what happens is that the eyes synthesize

90
00:05:27,200 --> 00:05:30,040
these slightly different photographs in such a way

91
00:05:30,140 --> 00:05:33,440
that it creates the illusion of motion.

92
00:05:33,540 --> 00:05:36,740
A popular early toy illustrates this synthesis

93
00:05:36,840 --> 00:05:39,240
or blending by the brain.

94
00:05:39,241 --> 00:05:42,080
When a card with a picture of a bird on one side

95
00:05:42,180 --> 00:05:45,820
and a picture of a bird cage on the other is twirled rapidly,

96
00:05:45,920 --> 00:05:47,720
the brain combines the two pictures,

97
00:05:47,820 --> 00:05:51,220
creating the illusion that the bird is in the cage.

98
00:05:55,120 --> 00:05:57,320
The zoetrope and many devices like it

99
00:05:57,420 --> 00:06:01,900
became popular in 19th century parlor amusements.

100
00:06:02,000 --> 00:06:05,860
But in 1829, a vital step in the evolution of the motion

101
00:06:05,960 --> 00:06:07,000
picture took place.

102
00:06:09,240 --> 00:06:14,240
Louis Daguerre perfected a truly magical technology, photography.

103
00:06:14,340 --> 00:06:18,640
Now, for the first time, a glimpse of the real world

104
00:06:18,740 --> 00:06:20,680
had been captured forever, frozen

105
00:06:20,780 --> 00:06:24,580
on a light-sensitive glass plate.

106
00:06:24,680 --> 00:06:28,500
But creating early photographs took as long as five minutes

107
00:06:28,600 --> 00:06:32,500
per image, too slow for movies.

108
00:06:32,600 --> 00:06:37,140
By the 1870s, faster picture-taking was possible.

109
00:06:37,141 --> 00:06:41,740
This allowed Edward Muybridge studying the movement of animals

110
00:06:41,840 --> 00:06:46,580
to make another major step toward motion pictures.

111
00:06:46,680 --> 00:06:50,820
California railroad magnate Leland Stanford hired

112
00:06:50,920 --> 00:06:53,320
Muybridge to prove a bet.

113
00:06:53,420 --> 00:06:57,620
Did all four hooves of a running horse ever leave the ground

114
00:06:57,720 --> 00:06:58,800
at the same time?

115
00:07:01,540 --> 00:07:04,500
Working at Stanford's Palo Alto Farm,

116
00:07:04,501 --> 00:07:10,340
Muybridge lined up a series of cameras along a short track.

117
00:07:10,440 --> 00:07:12,780
Threads stretched across the track were attached

118
00:07:12,880 --> 00:07:16,580
to each of the camera shutters.

119
00:07:16,680 --> 00:07:20,180
When a horse raced by, it broke the threads, which in turn

120
00:07:20,280 --> 00:07:24,760
released the camera shutters one after another.

121
00:07:24,860 --> 00:07:30,600
The result was a series of sequential photographs.

122
00:07:30,601 --> 00:07:34,700
Sure enough, there was a time when all four of the horse's

123
00:07:34,800 --> 00:07:36,540
legs were off the ground.

124
00:07:36,640 --> 00:07:39,000
But more important, Muybridge painted

125
00:07:39,100 --> 00:07:41,620
copies of his photographs on a glass wheel

126
00:07:41,720 --> 00:07:45,580
and created a viewing device called the zoopraxoscope.

127
00:07:45,680 --> 00:07:48,720
For the first time, there was a photographically accurate

128
00:07:48,820 --> 00:07:50,820
illusion of movement.

129
00:07:50,920 --> 00:07:53,660
When Thomas Edison heard of Muybridge's work,

130
00:07:53,760 --> 00:07:56,620
he was intrigued.

131
00:07:56,621 --> 00:08:00,420
In 1888, he and Muybridge met.

132
00:08:00,520 --> 00:08:02,420
Who knows what they talked about entirely,

133
00:08:02,520 --> 00:08:04,400
but certainly one thing they did talk about

134
00:08:04,500 --> 00:08:07,200
was how to possibly link the zoopraxiscope

135
00:08:07,300 --> 00:08:09,560
with Edison's phonograph.

136
00:08:09,660 --> 00:08:12,800
Edison had sort of found, to his surprise,

137
00:08:12,900 --> 00:08:15,900
that some of his competitors had made major innovations

138
00:08:16,000 --> 00:08:20,740
on his tinfoil phonograph, and he was trying to one-up them.

139
00:08:20,840 --> 00:08:23,280
Photographs that moved and talked,

140
00:08:23,380 --> 00:08:26,620
now that would shake up the competition.

141
00:08:26,621 --> 00:08:29,820
But work on movies didn't begin immediately.

142
00:08:29,920 --> 00:08:33,320
Edison's restless mind was elsewhere.

143
00:08:33,420 --> 00:08:36,260
At a mine he owned in nearby Ogdensburg,

144
00:08:36,360 --> 00:08:39,360
he was focused on a little known obsession of his life,

145
00:08:39,460 --> 00:08:45,380
a machine to separate iron ore with large magnets.

146
00:08:45,480 --> 00:08:51,580
It was typical of Edison to work on many ideas at the same time.

147
00:08:51,680 --> 00:08:56,620
In 1888, moving pictures was added to his list.

148
00:08:56,621 --> 00:09:00,060
To help, he chose a trusted assistant from the ore mining

149
00:09:00,160 --> 00:09:03,320
project, William Kennedy Laurie Dixon,

150
00:09:03,420 --> 00:09:06,400
an avid photographer who took most of the pictures

151
00:09:06,500 --> 00:09:10,740
around the lab, including this famous portrait of Edison

152
00:09:10,840 --> 00:09:12,840
as the Napoleon of invention.

153
00:09:16,000 --> 00:09:19,040
The motion picture project was perfect for Dixon.

154
00:09:19,140 --> 00:09:22,280
From the beginning, Edison conceived of a motion picture

155
00:09:22,380 --> 00:09:25,180
machine linked to the phonograph.

156
00:09:25,181 --> 00:09:28,680
sound and pictures together.

157
00:09:28,780 --> 00:09:33,080
On October 8, 1888, he wrote a caveat or preliminary

158
00:09:33,180 --> 00:09:36,720
announcement of his work in progress.

159
00:09:36,820 --> 00:09:39,520
I am experimenting upon an instrument which

160
00:09:39,620 --> 00:09:41,940
does for the eye what the phonograph

161
00:09:42,040 --> 00:09:46,300
does for the ear, which is the recording and reproduction

162
00:09:46,400 --> 00:09:47,940
of things in motion.

163
00:09:51,440 --> 00:09:53,840
The new invention was called the kinetoscope

164
00:09:53,841 --> 00:09:58,220
from the Greek words for motion and view.

165
00:09:58,320 --> 00:10:01,420
Dixon's job was to turn this exotic name

166
00:10:01,520 --> 00:10:02,720
into a working machine.

167
00:10:05,720 --> 00:10:09,160
With time, his role changed from assistant to collaborator

168
00:10:09,260 --> 00:10:14,400
to some say unaccredited co-inventor.

169
00:10:14,500 --> 00:10:19,600
Edison literally was not present in West Orange

170
00:10:19,700 --> 00:10:21,240
five or six days of the week.

171
00:10:21,241 --> 00:10:27,480
he was up in Northwestern Jersey on his iron ore mine.

172
00:10:27,580 --> 00:10:30,320
Dixon was the proprietor of the lab,

173
00:10:30,420 --> 00:10:33,560
and he was the man who was working most closely

174
00:10:33,660 --> 00:10:38,260
on developing the technology for the motion picture.

175
00:10:38,360 --> 00:10:41,120
Edison's first idea was to wrap a piece of film

176
00:10:41,220 --> 00:10:46,360
on a cylinder and expose a spiral of tiny photographs.

177
00:10:46,460 --> 00:10:48,040
But to fit on a phonograph cylinder,

178
00:10:48,041 --> 00:10:52,880
Dixon needed to make the pictures extremely small.

179
00:10:52,980 --> 00:10:55,840
Produced sometime in 1889, these are frames

180
00:10:55,940 --> 00:10:58,520
from the earliest surviving motion picture,

181
00:10:58,620 --> 00:11:02,660
nicknamed monkey shines.

182
00:11:02,760 --> 00:11:06,700
To see them, a viewer had to look through a microscope.

183
00:11:06,800 --> 00:11:10,220
Here, the individual frames have been reconstructed

184
00:11:10,320 --> 00:11:12,760
into movement.

185
00:11:12,860 --> 00:11:15,960
An agile pipe fitter from the island of Malta

186
00:11:15,961 --> 00:11:18,900
was drafted to star.

187
00:11:19,000 --> 00:11:22,760
Lasting only a few seconds, the image was crude

188
00:11:22,860 --> 00:11:25,400
and the sound now lost, unintelligible.

189
00:11:29,800 --> 00:11:32,580
Still by the end of 1890, after centuries

190
00:11:32,680 --> 00:11:36,180
of searching, false starts, and dead ends,

191
00:11:36,280 --> 00:11:40,480
the dream of moving pictures was about to be realized.

192
00:11:40,580 --> 00:11:44,720
But Edison and Dixon weren't the only men nearing

193
00:11:44,721 --> 00:11:45,960
nearing the solution.

194
00:11:50,760 --> 00:11:53,760
When Thomas Edison began experimenting with motion

195
00:11:53,860 --> 00:11:58,200
pictures in 1888, he wasn't alone.

196
00:11:58,300 --> 00:12:00,240
In the later part of the 19th century,

197
00:12:00,340 --> 00:12:03,300
there were a whole series of technological innovations that

198
00:12:03,400 --> 00:12:06,740
really made the cinema, made modern motion pictures

199
00:12:06,840 --> 00:12:07,740
possible.

200
00:12:07,840 --> 00:12:11,520
And there were an array of people in Europe, in France,

201
00:12:11,620 --> 00:12:13,580
in England, in Germany, in the United States,

202
00:12:13,581 --> 00:12:15,820
who were experimenting with these elements

203
00:12:15,920 --> 00:12:17,820
and trying to pull them together in things that

204
00:12:17,920 --> 00:12:20,320
resembled motion pictures.

205
00:12:20,420 --> 00:12:24,060
In France, there was Etienne Jeune Marais.

206
00:12:24,160 --> 00:12:26,200
Like Edison, Marais was influenced

207
00:12:26,300 --> 00:12:28,900
by Edward Muybridge's work.

208
00:12:29,000 --> 00:12:32,100
He too was interested in studying the movements of animals

209
00:12:32,200 --> 00:12:35,100
with photography.

210
00:12:35,200 --> 00:12:37,880
But unlike Muybridge's multi-camera setup,

211
00:12:37,980 --> 00:12:39,900
he developed a single camera system.

212
00:12:43,580 --> 00:12:45,780
Moray's camera looked like a rifle,

213
00:12:45,781 --> 00:12:48,560
but the cylinders of his photographic gun

214
00:12:48,561 --> 00:12:50,580
contained lenses, not bullets.

215
00:12:53,200 --> 00:12:56,020
When he pulled the trigger, the cylinder revolved,

216
00:12:56,021 --> 00:12:58,860
taking sequential photographs on a strip of film.

217
00:13:01,840 --> 00:13:04,860
In 1889, with his cylinder motion picture work

218
00:13:04,861 --> 00:13:08,740
approaching a dead end, Edison decided to go to Paris

219
00:13:08,741 --> 00:13:11,780
for an exhibition marking the 50th anniversary

220
00:13:11,781 --> 00:13:15,480
of the invention of photography.

221
00:13:15,580 --> 00:13:19,120
At the exhibition, Edison's display of electrical marvels

222
00:13:19,220 --> 00:13:24,060
was a Parisian triumph, as memorable as the new Eiffel Tower.

223
00:13:26,860 --> 00:13:29,660
Motion pictures were probably far from his mind.

224
00:13:32,240 --> 00:13:34,640
Despite this, unknowingly, Edison

225
00:13:34,740 --> 00:13:38,340
was also approaching a turning point in his work toward true

226
00:13:38,440 --> 00:13:40,440
moving pictures.

227
00:13:40,441 --> 00:13:44,580
In Paris, he met many scientists and researchers.

228
00:13:44,581 --> 00:13:48,840
Etienne Jeune Marais, the inventor of the photographic gun,

229
00:13:48,841 --> 00:13:50,640
was one of them.

230
00:13:50,641 --> 00:13:52,960
It's very important to remember this

231
00:13:52,961 --> 00:13:55,320
in terms of understanding Edison's

232
00:13:55,321 --> 00:13:58,280
successful inventive process.

233
00:13:58,281 --> 00:14:03,780
He did not get these ideas simply out of some corner

234
00:14:03,781 --> 00:14:05,560
recess in his brain.

235
00:14:05,561 --> 00:14:07,460
He read widely.

236
00:14:07,461 --> 00:14:09,980
He attempted to find out what everybody else

237
00:14:09,981 --> 00:14:13,120
was doing in order to incorporate these ideas

238
00:14:13,121 --> 00:14:17,020
and give them a new twist.

239
00:14:17,021 --> 00:14:20,060
Back in America in October 1889,

240
00:14:20,061 --> 00:14:24,420
Edison changed the direction of his motion picture research.

241
00:14:24,421 --> 00:14:27,460
He abandoned the cylinder concept.

242
00:14:27,461 --> 00:14:30,640
Instead, he wanted perforated strips of film,

243
00:14:30,641 --> 00:14:34,940
a variation of Moray's idea.

244
00:14:34,941 --> 00:14:38,540
Edison's assistant W.K.L. Dixon was soon at work

245
00:14:38,541 --> 00:14:42,140
on a new motion picture machine.

246
00:14:42,240 --> 00:14:44,320
Unlike today's cameras and projectors,

247
00:14:44,420 --> 00:14:49,020
the film moved laterally through the mechanism, not top to bottom.

248
00:14:49,120 --> 00:14:51,720
In March of 1890, Dixon was ready to show

249
00:14:51,820 --> 00:14:54,080
his preliminary handiwork.

250
00:14:54,180 --> 00:14:57,060
Edison was impressed.

251
00:14:57,160 --> 00:14:58,160
That's it, he said.

252
00:14:58,260 --> 00:14:59,700
Work like hell.

253
00:14:59,800 --> 00:15:01,460
Dixon did.

254
00:15:01,560 --> 00:15:04,640
Working with his partner machinist Thomas Heiss,

255
00:15:04,641 --> 00:15:11,840
The two are seen on a frame from an 1892 experimental film.

256
00:15:11,841 --> 00:15:17,200
Together, they were inventing motion pictures.

257
00:15:17,201 --> 00:15:20,960
They started with film strips 3 quarter inches wide,

258
00:15:20,961 --> 00:15:23,520
moving later to 1 and 1 half inch strips,

259
00:15:23,521 --> 00:15:28,040
close to today's standard of 35 millimeter.

260
00:15:28,041 --> 00:15:32,880
Sprocket holes were punched with a manual perforator.

261
00:15:32,881 --> 00:15:37,820
On May 20th, 1891, Dixon had made enough progress

262
00:15:37,920 --> 00:15:39,420
for a surprise premiere.

263
00:15:42,660 --> 00:15:45,800
On that day, a group of women were visiting Edison's wife,

264
00:15:45,900 --> 00:15:48,460
Mina, at Glenmont.

265
00:15:48,560 --> 00:15:51,340
They were ushered into the lab, where one by one, they were

266
00:15:51,440 --> 00:15:52,940
asked to look into a wooden box.

267
00:15:55,900 --> 00:15:58,880
Inside, they saw something startling,

268
00:15:58,980 --> 00:16:00,740
a photograph of Dixon that moved.

269
00:16:02,880 --> 00:16:08,480
The little black and white image gallantly removed his straw hat.

270
00:16:08,580 --> 00:16:13,520
These are the few remaining frames of that landmark movie.

271
00:16:13,620 --> 00:16:16,160
Minus luncheon guests were the first outsiders

272
00:16:16,260 --> 00:16:20,740
to see the still experimental kinetoscope in action.

273
00:16:20,840 --> 00:16:25,140
And Edison's little surprise wasn't a casual decision.

274
00:16:25,240 --> 00:16:28,940
Edison was aware that he was working against a whole group

275
00:16:29,040 --> 00:16:30,040
of would-be competitors.

276
00:16:30,041 --> 00:16:33,940
And so I think as soon as an experiment was perfected

277
00:16:34,040 --> 00:16:37,840
or even able to be shown, he would find an opportunity

278
00:16:37,940 --> 00:16:39,980
or an excuse to show it to a public,

279
00:16:40,080 --> 00:16:43,680
because that became key evidence in patent battles.

280
00:16:43,780 --> 00:16:46,060
But he would always maintain that it had been around for a long

281
00:16:46,160 --> 00:16:49,020
time so that if someone came up with an earlier date,

282
00:16:49,120 --> 00:16:50,520
he could fudge it a little bit.

283
00:16:50,620 --> 00:16:54,000
And he, in fact, was the master of fudging dates.

284
00:16:54,100 --> 00:16:56,760
Despite its private debut for Mina's friends,

285
00:16:56,860 --> 00:17:00,040
the kinetoscope still needed work.

286
00:17:00,041 --> 00:17:04,780
The images were blurry and the action jerky.

287
00:17:04,880 --> 00:17:07,680
But instead of rushing to improve his invention,

288
00:17:07,780 --> 00:17:10,780
Edison turned again to other work.

289
00:17:10,880 --> 00:17:15,380
It seemed like he was always juggling too many projects.

290
00:17:17,600 --> 00:17:21,660
This notebook contains strips of experimental film side

291
00:17:21,760 --> 00:17:26,140
by side with notes for Edison's ore mining project.

292
00:17:26,141 --> 00:17:29,640
It wasn't until a year later, July 1891,

293
00:17:29,740 --> 00:17:33,380
that he applied for the kinetoscope's first basic patent.

294
00:17:35,440 --> 00:17:41,880
Only two years later, in 1893, Edison's fortunes were down.

295
00:17:41,980 --> 00:17:46,100
He was in need of another money-making marvel.

296
00:17:46,200 --> 00:17:49,000
He decided to announce the kinetoscope at the Columbia

297
00:17:49,100 --> 00:17:53,900
Exposition in Chicago, but the machine was far from finished.

298
00:17:53,901 --> 00:17:58,500
Forced to divide his time, W.K.L. Dixon worked feverishly

299
00:17:58,600 --> 00:18:02,640
to perfect the bulky mechanism.

300
00:18:02,740 --> 00:18:06,540
In the process, he worked as designer, engineer,

301
00:18:06,640 --> 00:18:11,960
projectionist, and often actor in the early films.

302
00:18:12,060 --> 00:18:16,560
Here, he plays the violin into a phonograph recording horn.

303
00:18:16,660 --> 00:18:19,100
The sound has been lost.

304
00:18:19,200 --> 00:18:23,900
A means to synchronize sound in pictures remained elusive.

305
00:18:23,901 --> 00:18:29,200
The pressure on Dixon kept building until he snapped.

306
00:18:29,300 --> 00:18:31,680
Dixon ended up with a nervous breakdown.

307
00:18:31,780 --> 00:18:35,240
He was pursuing too many of Edison's ideas simultaneously.

308
00:18:35,340 --> 00:18:37,140
The work schedule was insane.

309
00:18:37,240 --> 00:18:38,980
He collapsed and had to go down to Florida

310
00:18:39,080 --> 00:18:40,480
for a number of months.

311
00:18:40,580 --> 00:18:42,580
The deadline for the Columbia Exposition

312
00:18:42,680 --> 00:18:44,860
came and went.

313
00:18:44,960 --> 00:18:48,360
It was a major opportunity lost.

314
00:18:48,460 --> 00:18:51,560
But Edison still seemed unsure about the value

315
00:18:51,561 --> 00:18:54,060
of his moving image machine.

316
00:18:54,160 --> 00:18:58,040
In 1893, he wrote to Edward Muybridge,

317
00:18:58,140 --> 00:19:02,340
I have constructed a little instrument I call the kinetograph,

318
00:19:02,440 --> 00:19:06,380
but I'm very doubtful if there's any commercial feature in it.

319
00:19:09,020 --> 00:19:10,920
Edison had missed his opportunity

320
00:19:11,020 --> 00:19:14,220
to show he was first with the motion picture machine,

321
00:19:14,320 --> 00:19:16,280
but there were others close behind.

322
00:19:16,281 --> 00:19:17,180
behind.

323
00:19:20,580 --> 00:19:26,020
The main theme of the 1893 Columbia Exposition in Chicago

324
00:19:26,120 --> 00:19:28,220
was electricity.

325
00:19:28,320 --> 00:19:32,020
Exposition halls were filled with the giant dynamos spawned

326
00:19:32,120 --> 00:19:36,000
by Edison's creation of the first electric lighting system

327
00:19:36,100 --> 00:19:39,440
in 1882.

328
00:19:39,540 --> 00:19:42,740
Edison was chosen as the living American who would most be

329
00:19:42,741 --> 00:19:48,080
remembered 100 years later, 1993.

330
00:19:48,180 --> 00:19:52,020
Not far from the truth, but Edison's most recent invention,

331
00:19:52,120 --> 00:19:57,420
a moving pictures machine, was nowhere to be seen.

332
00:19:57,520 --> 00:20:01,960
Later in 1893, anxious to demonstrate his lead,

333
00:20:02,060 --> 00:20:04,560
Edison held a public showing of the kinetoscope

334
00:20:04,660 --> 00:20:07,740
for a group of scientists at the Brooklyn Institute of Arts

335
00:20:07,840 --> 00:20:10,700
and Sciences.

336
00:20:10,701 --> 00:20:14,040
The film he showed was one of Dixon's many movies,

337
00:20:14,140 --> 00:20:15,140
The Blacksmith Shop.

338
00:20:18,200 --> 00:20:20,140
These are two frames from that film.

339
00:20:23,040 --> 00:20:26,680
In 1894, Dixon's health had improved,

340
00:20:26,780 --> 00:20:28,020
and he was back in the lab.

341
00:20:31,080 --> 00:20:37,520
His first assignment, design and build, the first movie studio.

342
00:20:37,521 --> 00:20:42,660
Constructed at a cost of $638, covered with black tar paper,

343
00:20:42,760 --> 00:20:46,320
it resembled a 19th century police patrol wagon

344
00:20:46,420 --> 00:20:50,060
and was called the Black Mariah.

345
00:20:50,160 --> 00:20:52,860
A model shows how a section of the building's roof

346
00:20:52,960 --> 00:20:56,040
opened to let in the light, and the whole structure turned

347
00:20:56,140 --> 00:21:00,840
on a rail track so that it could follow the sun.

348
00:21:00,940 --> 00:21:02,920
Early film needed bright sunlight

349
00:21:03,020 --> 00:21:05,340
to produce clear images.

350
00:21:05,341 --> 00:21:08,740
Making movies in the Black Mariah was slow and hot.

351
00:21:12,040 --> 00:21:13,980
Working with the bulky equipment,

352
00:21:14,080 --> 00:21:19,320
it often took Dixon a week to get 15 seconds of usable film.

353
00:21:19,420 --> 00:21:22,920
This is the kind of camera he used, called a kinetograph.

354
00:21:25,800 --> 00:21:28,840
One of the first movies produced in Dixon's Black Mariah

355
00:21:28,940 --> 00:21:31,340
studio starred a coworker, Fred Ott.

356
00:21:31,341 --> 00:21:34,240
The Sneeze.

357
00:21:34,340 --> 00:21:37,740
It was short and to the point.

358
00:21:40,680 --> 00:21:44,280
In 1894, with an application signed by Dixon

359
00:21:44,380 --> 00:21:46,860
and submitted with a 50 cent fee,

360
00:21:46,960 --> 00:21:50,060
the Edison Kinetoscopic Study of the Sneeze

361
00:21:50,160 --> 00:21:53,860
was the first film to be copyrighted with the Library

362
00:21:53,960 --> 00:21:54,800
of Congress.

363
00:21:57,300 --> 00:22:00,300
Hardly born, the movies began taking their first time

364
00:22:00,301 --> 00:22:03,900
taking their first full strides.

365
00:22:04,000 --> 00:22:06,940
The day would come when a single film would cost

366
00:22:07,040 --> 00:22:10,440
over $100 million.

367
00:22:10,540 --> 00:22:13,440
Edison's account books show that the entire process

368
00:22:13,540 --> 00:22:20,260
of creating the first kinetoscope was $24,118.04,

369
00:22:20,360 --> 00:22:24,060
about $200,000 today.

370
00:22:24,160 --> 00:22:26,700
Edison never avoided a profit.

371
00:22:26,800 --> 00:22:29,400
But despite Dixon's progress, he continued

372
00:22:29,401 --> 00:22:34,500
to delay commercial release of the kinetoscope.

373
00:22:34,600 --> 00:22:39,140
Without sound, he wasn't satisfied.

374
00:22:39,240 --> 00:22:42,580
It took others in his company to push the silent kinetoscope

375
00:22:42,680 --> 00:22:45,720
into the marketplace.

376
00:22:45,820 --> 00:22:49,620
In 1894, a group of outside investors

377
00:22:49,720 --> 00:22:54,520
bought 25 kinetoscopes and set up shop with 10 machines

378
00:22:54,620 --> 00:22:57,320
at 1155 Broadway, New York City.

379
00:22:59,400 --> 00:23:05,540
Edison made sure he got hefty royalties from their efforts.

380
00:23:05,640 --> 00:23:11,140
The first movie premiere was April 14, 1894.

381
00:23:11,240 --> 00:23:15,140
The Kinetoscope was an instant sensation.

382
00:23:15,240 --> 00:23:18,960
Soon, Kinetoscope parlors were all the rage.

383
00:23:19,060 --> 00:23:21,660
An early special event was a boxing match

384
00:23:21,760 --> 00:23:24,460
between former champ, Gentleman Jim Corbett,

385
00:23:24,560 --> 00:23:29,400
and a punching bag challenger named Pete Courtney.

386
00:23:29,401 --> 00:23:33,600
Sweating in the impressive heat of the Black Mariah,

387
00:23:33,700 --> 00:23:37,300
Dixon shouted instructions to make sure Corbett knocked out

388
00:23:37,400 --> 00:23:40,640
his opponent during the appointed sixth round

389
00:23:40,740 --> 00:23:45,640
and in a spot where the action could be in focus.

390
00:23:45,740 --> 00:23:48,660
In kinetoscope parlors, one round of the fight

391
00:23:48,760 --> 00:23:51,160
was assigned to each of six machines,

392
00:23:51,260 --> 00:23:55,460
and viewers paid 50 cents to move from machine to machine

393
00:23:55,461 --> 00:23:58,200
until they reached kinetoscope number six

394
00:23:58,300 --> 00:24:02,100
and the climactic knockout.

395
00:24:02,200 --> 00:24:06,200
Other less violent fare also attracted predominantly male

396
00:24:06,300 --> 00:24:10,500
audiences, like the risque Carmencita's dance.

397
00:24:10,600 --> 00:24:14,220
The movie certainly didn't begin as high-class entertainment,

398
00:24:14,320 --> 00:24:17,520
but they reflected Edison's down-to-earth taste.

399
00:24:17,620 --> 00:24:20,560
After the Black Mariah was built,

400
00:24:20,660 --> 00:24:24,060
you began to see a stream of entertainers,

401
00:24:24,061 --> 00:24:28,600
entertainers, vaudeville acts, the world's strong man of the time,

402
00:24:28,601 --> 00:24:32,240
Eugene Sandow, come to the laboratory at West Orange

403
00:24:32,241 --> 00:24:34,140
to perform in front of the camera.

404
00:24:34,141 --> 00:24:35,300
Edison would go out.

405
00:24:35,301 --> 00:24:37,460
He would horse around with Sandow.

406
00:24:37,461 --> 00:24:40,440
The reporters were there every time one of these films were

407
00:24:40,441 --> 00:24:42,420
taken, and they'd write headlines like,

408
00:24:42,421 --> 00:24:45,880
world's smartest man meets world's strongest man.

409
00:24:45,881 --> 00:24:49,380
Everybody had a good time, and the lab staff got free passes

410
00:24:49,381 --> 00:24:52,440
to Broadway shows.

411
00:24:52,441 --> 00:24:54,780
As simple as the early movies were,

412
00:24:54,781 --> 00:24:58,780
the power of the new medium quickly made itself known,

413
00:24:58,781 --> 00:25:02,500
pushing 19th century proprieties.

414
00:25:02,501 --> 00:25:06,940
This film was called Her First Cigarette.

415
00:25:06,941 --> 00:25:09,460
These early kinetoscope films, in important ways,

416
00:25:09,461 --> 00:25:12,680
had a major impact on American culture right from the start.

417
00:25:12,681 --> 00:25:14,880
A lot of these films were of things

418
00:25:14,881 --> 00:25:18,560
that were really forbidden to be seen in the flesh.

419
00:25:18,561 --> 00:25:20,140
His motion pictures really from the outset

420
00:25:20,141 --> 00:25:23,140
of turned commercial popular culture on its ear.

421
00:25:23,240 --> 00:25:26,780
I mean, he was, it was a very disruptive force.

422
00:25:26,880 --> 00:25:31,420
By 1895, kinetoscope audiences were increasing.

423
00:25:31,520 --> 00:25:33,620
But Edison's partner, Dixon, wanted

424
00:25:33,720 --> 00:25:36,260
to take the invention one step further

425
00:25:36,360 --> 00:25:40,860
and project the images on a screen.

426
00:25:40,960 --> 00:25:43,700
Edison vehemently disagreed.

427
00:25:43,800 --> 00:25:49,000
Why kill the goose that lays the golden egg, he insisted.

428
00:25:49,001 --> 00:25:52,800
If he felt that you were infringing upon him in any way,

429
00:25:52,900 --> 00:25:57,780
questioning his authority, questioning his integrity

430
00:25:57,880 --> 00:26:01,220
or his imagination, differing with the direction that he

431
00:26:01,320 --> 00:26:04,280
thought should be taken in a given project,

432
00:26:04,380 --> 00:26:12,320
then he could become very contrarian, arbitrary, angry.

433
00:26:12,420 --> 00:26:16,420
He could stop speaking to you for 25 years.

434
00:26:16,421 --> 00:26:19,920
Whatever Edison thought, ambitious entrepreneurs

435
00:26:20,020 --> 00:26:22,220
weren't waiting for the wizard to come up

436
00:26:22,320 --> 00:26:25,020
with a projecting kinetoscope.

437
00:26:25,120 --> 00:26:28,560
Frustrated with lab managers and eager to innovate,

438
00:26:28,660 --> 00:26:33,640
W.K.L. Dixon started working on a projection system of his own,

439
00:26:33,740 --> 00:26:36,480
in secret with someone else.

440
00:26:40,480 --> 00:26:44,340
In 1895, frustrated with his boss's refusal

441
00:26:44,341 --> 00:26:49,380
to develop a projection system, Edison project manager W.K.L.

442
00:26:49,480 --> 00:26:52,580
Dixon had begun working with Edison competitors.

443
00:26:55,480 --> 00:26:57,580
When his activities were discovered,

444
00:26:57,680 --> 00:27:01,360
he was forced to leave the lab.

445
00:27:01,460 --> 00:27:05,120
Edison felt he'd been double-crossed.

446
00:27:05,220 --> 00:27:08,000
Dixon went on to help found the new movie company,

447
00:27:08,100 --> 00:27:09,340
American Mutoscope.

448
00:27:09,341 --> 00:27:14,340
As competition to the kinetoscope, American Mutoscope

449
00:27:14,440 --> 00:27:20,080
created a peep show machine that used images on file cards that

450
00:27:20,180 --> 00:27:22,880
flicked past the viewer.

451
00:27:22,980 --> 00:27:25,920
This simpler system was cheaper and more reliable

452
00:27:26,020 --> 00:27:29,020
than the film-based kinetoscope.

453
00:27:29,120 --> 00:27:32,000
At the same time, Dixon and his partners pushed ahead

454
00:27:32,100 --> 00:27:33,700
on a projection system.

455
00:27:33,701 --> 00:27:39,000
It was a worldwide race.

456
00:27:39,100 --> 00:27:43,340
In France, there were two brothers, Auguste and Louis

457
00:27:43,440 --> 00:27:47,340
Lumiere, owners of a successful photographic equipment business.

458
00:27:50,460 --> 00:27:52,460
Their Lumiere motion picture machine

459
00:27:52,560 --> 00:27:54,760
was called the Cinematograph.

460
00:27:54,860 --> 00:27:58,600
Light and portable, unlike Edison and Dixon's bulky

461
00:27:58,700 --> 00:28:02,600
apparatus, the Cinematograph's elegantly designed cameras

462
00:28:02,601 --> 00:28:07,400
designed camera projector also reduced Edison's 46 frames

463
00:28:07,500 --> 00:28:12,340
per second speed to 16 frames for quieter, less jittery

464
00:28:12,440 --> 00:28:14,180
projection.

465
00:28:14,280 --> 00:28:17,740
Also, rather than using bulky electric power,

466
00:28:17,840 --> 00:28:20,920
the Lumiere camera was hand-cranked, ready to go

467
00:28:21,020 --> 00:28:23,080
anywhere.

468
00:28:23,180 --> 00:28:26,920
This is one of the early Lumiere films.

469
00:28:27,020 --> 00:28:31,000
They were first projected on a large screen on December 28,

470
00:28:31,001 --> 00:28:33,540
1895.

471
00:28:33,640 --> 00:28:37,040
Taking advantage of the cinematograph's portability,

472
00:28:37,140 --> 00:28:40,040
Lumiere films delighted audiences with scenes

473
00:28:40,140 --> 00:28:46,080
from everyday life, in contrast to Edison's vaudeville

474
00:28:46,180 --> 00:28:47,820
acts and stage presentations.

475
00:28:50,380 --> 00:28:53,660
Back in America, a few weeks ahead of the Lumieres,

476
00:28:53,760 --> 00:28:57,920
Charles Jenkins and Thomas Armat demonstrated another projection

477
00:28:57,921 --> 00:29:00,320
in September of 1895.

478
00:29:04,420 --> 00:29:07,460
They called their projector the Fantascope.

479
00:29:07,560 --> 00:29:10,400
By rapidly starting and stopping the film as it passed

480
00:29:10,500 --> 00:29:12,700
through the projector, the Fantascope

481
00:29:12,800 --> 00:29:16,900
eliminated the annoying flicker in Edison's continuous motion

482
00:29:17,000 --> 00:29:19,440
kinetoscope.

483
00:29:19,540 --> 00:29:22,180
Edison was stubborn.

484
00:29:22,280 --> 00:29:25,280
It was usually his way or not at all.

485
00:29:25,281 --> 00:29:28,720
But with audiences demanding movies on a screen,

486
00:29:28,820 --> 00:29:33,020
he was finally convinced to put his name on a projector,

487
00:29:33,120 --> 00:29:34,520
not his.

488
00:29:34,620 --> 00:29:38,620
He licensed the machine invented by Jenkins and Armat.

489
00:29:38,720 --> 00:29:42,640
They renamed it the Vitascope.

490
00:29:42,740 --> 00:29:45,240
The Vitascope motion picture projector

491
00:29:45,340 --> 00:29:51,380
debuted on April 23, 1896 at Koster and Beals Music Hall

492
00:29:51,480 --> 00:29:54,280
in New York City.

493
00:29:54,281 --> 00:29:57,320
Modern movies were born.

494
00:29:57,420 --> 00:30:01,760
For audiences, it was love at first sight.

495
00:30:01,860 --> 00:30:08,960
The most impressive short film was of surf crashing

496
00:30:09,060 --> 00:30:11,160
onto the shore at Dover.

497
00:30:11,260 --> 00:30:14,400
And this was a film loop that was repeated over and over again.

498
00:30:14,500 --> 00:30:17,000
And a number of people in the audience so struck

499
00:30:17,100 --> 00:30:19,440
by the realism of this dived under their seats,

500
00:30:19,540 --> 00:30:22,200
thinking that they were going to get hit by a wave.

501
00:30:22,201 --> 00:30:26,400
Unacknowledged in the back of the house, Thomas Armand,

502
00:30:26,500 --> 00:30:29,300
one of the vitascope's true inventors,

503
00:30:29,400 --> 00:30:33,080
watched his machine making sure everything went well,

504
00:30:33,180 --> 00:30:38,180
while audiences cheered Edison's latest marvel.

505
00:30:38,280 --> 00:30:42,180
The Edison name meant box office.

506
00:30:42,280 --> 00:30:46,820
The Kiss was one of Edison's earliest hits.

507
00:30:46,920 --> 00:30:50,000
It recreated a scene from a popular Broadway show

508
00:30:50,001 --> 00:30:52,800
and had the further distinction of inspiring probably

509
00:30:52,900 --> 00:30:55,240
the first competitive movie remake.

510
00:31:00,280 --> 00:31:04,980
In April 1896, after six years, Edison's patent

511
00:31:05,080 --> 00:31:09,460
on the fundamental motion picture system was approved.

512
00:31:09,560 --> 00:31:14,000
But even that didn't stop the competitive warfare.

513
00:31:14,100 --> 00:31:16,000
In Europe and the United States,

514
00:31:16,100 --> 00:31:20,000
alternative projecting machines were popping up everywhere.

515
00:31:20,001 --> 00:31:23,540
Everybody was suing the daylights out of everybody else,

516
00:31:23,640 --> 00:31:26,400
trying to promote their own competitive position.

517
00:31:26,500 --> 00:31:31,280
And everybody was probably guilty of shading the truth

518
00:31:31,380 --> 00:31:34,620
in terms of arguing what they had really done

519
00:31:34,720 --> 00:31:38,580
and making claims against their competitor.

520
00:31:38,680 --> 00:31:41,260
Not even legal turmoil could stop

521
00:31:41,360 --> 00:31:44,320
the growing influence of motion pictures.

522
00:31:46,460 --> 00:31:49,200
From storefronts, special places

523
00:31:49,201 --> 00:31:51,000
for movies evolved.

524
00:31:51,100 --> 00:31:53,940
They were called Nickelodeons.

525
00:31:54,040 --> 00:31:57,600
Combining peep show arcades with projection theaters,

526
00:31:57,700 --> 00:32:01,240
Nickelodeons began providing popular entertainment

527
00:32:01,340 --> 00:32:04,640
for middle class audiences.

528
00:32:04,740 --> 00:32:09,160
Next came theaters dedicated to projected films.

529
00:32:09,260 --> 00:32:14,160
One of the first was Tallies in Los Angeles.

530
00:32:14,260 --> 00:32:18,160
Early motion picture audiences were whisked around the world

531
00:32:18,161 --> 00:32:19,660
and across the country.

532
00:32:23,660 --> 00:32:28,300
Vanishing people and places were captured forever.

533
00:32:28,400 --> 00:32:33,200
Perceptions of time and distance had begun to shrink.

534
00:32:33,300 --> 00:32:36,920
It didn't take long for the power and influence of the movies

535
00:32:37,020 --> 00:32:39,920
to be appreciated.

536
00:32:40,020 --> 00:32:44,720
In 1898, W.K.L. Dixon, now living and working in England,

537
00:32:44,820 --> 00:32:47,120
scored another motion picture first

538
00:32:47,121 --> 00:32:51,320
when he was granted permission to film Pope Leo XIII

539
00:32:51,420 --> 00:32:52,520
in the Vatican.

540
00:32:56,660 --> 00:32:58,860
Dixon's career in Europe would eventually

541
00:32:58,960 --> 00:33:00,660
go into gradual decline.

542
00:33:00,760 --> 00:33:04,880
But in Paris, he proudly posed with fellow movie pioneer

543
00:33:04,980 --> 00:33:08,280
Etienne Jeune Marais.

544
00:33:08,380 --> 00:33:12,720
Back in America in 1900, the Edison Motion Picture Company

545
00:33:12,820 --> 00:33:16,020
gained another influential employee.

546
00:33:16,021 --> 00:33:19,720
His name, Edwin S. Porter.

547
00:33:19,820 --> 00:33:22,720
This 1902 Edison film made by Porter,

548
00:33:22,820 --> 00:33:25,660
Jack and the Beanstalk is typical of its time.

549
00:33:28,760 --> 00:33:32,780
All the action is taken from one unmoving position.

550
00:33:32,880 --> 00:33:36,280
Audiences were getting bored.

551
00:33:36,380 --> 00:33:40,480
Porter recognized that the movies need new forms.

552
00:33:40,580 --> 00:33:44,180
The question was how to find a new kind of subject matter

553
00:33:44,181 --> 00:33:46,620
that would get people back in the theaters.

554
00:33:46,720 --> 00:33:48,280
And the solution really was a simple one.

555
00:33:48,380 --> 00:33:50,920
It was the story film.

556
00:33:51,020 --> 00:33:55,420
A landmark in his development is this 1903 film Porter made

557
00:33:55,520 --> 00:33:59,400
for Edison, The Great Train Robbery.

558
00:33:59,500 --> 00:34:02,860
Here is certainly the first movie Western,

559
00:34:02,960 --> 00:34:05,940
although it's claimed to be the first film to tell a story

560
00:34:06,040 --> 00:34:10,280
could be challenged by European movies of the day.

561
00:34:10,380 --> 00:34:14,180
Still, Porter's film shows how far motion picture takes

562
00:34:14,181 --> 00:34:17,780
had come in little more than a decade,

563
00:34:17,880 --> 00:34:21,660
including special effects like the image of a train inserted

564
00:34:21,760 --> 00:34:25,260
in the station house window.

565
00:34:25,360 --> 00:34:27,900
With longer storytelling films, movies

566
00:34:28,000 --> 00:34:31,000
were even farther than ever from the first Kinetoscope

567
00:34:31,100 --> 00:34:32,460
entertainments.

568
00:34:32,560 --> 00:34:34,660
But despite Porter's example, Edison

569
00:34:34,760 --> 00:34:38,340
was slow to go into feature length film production.

570
00:34:38,440 --> 00:34:41,580
When Porter resisted making movies like Factory Products

571
00:34:41,581 --> 00:34:46,380
in 1909, Edison fired him.

572
00:34:46,480 --> 00:34:49,720
It was the beginning of the end for the Edison Motion Picture

573
00:34:49,820 --> 00:34:52,220
Company.

574
00:34:52,320 --> 00:34:56,320
Edison was never interested in being a film producer.

575
00:34:56,420 --> 00:35:00,400
In the end, he left it to others to turn an ingenious technology

576
00:35:00,500 --> 00:35:04,600
into the 20th century's most powerful communications

577
00:35:04,700 --> 00:35:05,600
medium.

578
00:35:05,601 --> 00:35:10,600
From short vignettes, by 1902, movies

579
00:35:10,700 --> 00:35:13,340
were exploring new forms.

580
00:35:15,440 --> 00:35:19,240
The film A Trip to the Moon represents an explosion

581
00:35:19,340 --> 00:35:20,740
of innovation.

582
00:35:22,360 --> 00:35:25,860
It's the work of French magician, filmmaker, Georges

583
00:35:25,960 --> 00:35:27,360
Melies.

584
00:35:27,460 --> 00:35:30,960
Even before Edison employee, Edwin S. Porter Melies

585
00:35:31,060 --> 00:35:33,200
and other French filmmakers were beginning

586
00:35:33,201 --> 00:35:36,200
were beginning to tell stories in motion pictures.

587
00:35:40,200 --> 00:35:42,200
Inspired by the Lumiere brothers,

588
00:35:42,300 --> 00:35:44,940
Milliers was creating dramas that could only

589
00:35:45,040 --> 00:35:46,840
be presented on film.

590
00:35:46,940 --> 00:35:48,840
It was a new means of storytelling

591
00:35:48,940 --> 00:35:52,060
and the beginning of a new art form.

592
00:35:52,160 --> 00:35:57,460
For Edison, there were opponents overseas as well as at home.

593
00:35:57,560 --> 00:35:59,760
Edison was always trying to defend his position.

594
00:35:59,860 --> 00:36:02,360
After all, he started off as the only motion picture producer

595
00:36:02,361 --> 00:36:03,460
producer in the world.

596
00:36:03,560 --> 00:36:04,960
Pretty soon the French were coming in.

597
00:36:05,060 --> 00:36:07,100
Then he had a whole series of American competitors.

598
00:36:07,200 --> 00:36:09,400
He always felt he was on the verge of sort of being

599
00:36:09,500 --> 00:36:11,000
marginalized, being put out of business.

600
00:36:11,100 --> 00:36:12,700
And he was trying to defend himself

601
00:36:12,800 --> 00:36:13,900
and his commercial interests.

602
00:36:14,000 --> 00:36:16,240
And of course, one of his greatest strengths

603
00:36:16,340 --> 00:36:18,240
was his patents.

604
00:36:18,340 --> 00:36:22,040
In 1909, in the midst of continuing industry warfare

605
00:36:22,140 --> 00:36:25,880
for mutual survival, Edison and his biggest competitors

606
00:36:25,980 --> 00:36:28,820
decided to call a truce.

607
00:36:28,920 --> 00:36:32,360
They joined together in the Motion Pictures Patent Company.

608
00:36:32,361 --> 00:36:35,660
an attempt to wrest control of a movie business running wild

609
00:36:35,760 --> 00:36:41,440
with foreign companies and independent entrepreneurs.

610
00:36:41,540 --> 00:36:44,500
Hired enforcers raided non-patent company

611
00:36:44,600 --> 00:36:49,840
productions, sometimes shooting holes through bootleg cameras.

612
00:36:49,940 --> 00:36:52,480
Kodak's George Eastman joined Edison

613
00:36:52,580 --> 00:36:57,420
and refused to sell film stock to independents.

614
00:36:57,520 --> 00:37:00,780
Such actions led producers to move farther away from New

615
00:37:00,781 --> 00:37:03,280
and New Jersey, where most movies were made.

616
00:37:05,580 --> 00:37:10,320
Eventually, many settled in a rural town near Los Angeles.

617
00:37:10,420 --> 00:37:15,260
The area had good climate, varied location possibilities,

618
00:37:15,360 --> 00:37:18,860
and also it was convenient to the Mexican border

619
00:37:18,960 --> 00:37:22,500
in case of trouble from Edison's patent men.

620
00:37:22,600 --> 00:37:26,040
The new film center was called Hollywood.

621
00:37:26,140 --> 00:37:28,680
Finally, after years of court fights,

622
00:37:28,681 --> 00:37:32,280
independent producers William Fox and Carl Lemley,

623
00:37:32,380 --> 00:37:35,220
founder of Universal Studios, won.

624
00:37:38,160 --> 00:37:43,420
But by that time, Thomas Edison was no longer a movie producer.

625
00:37:43,520 --> 00:37:46,500
In 1911, before he left movies to others,

626
00:37:46,600 --> 00:37:50,200
Edison made one last attempt at an old dream,

627
00:37:50,300 --> 00:37:55,140
motion pictures with synchronized sound.

628
00:37:55,141 --> 00:37:58,180
Silent films were never really silent.

629
00:37:58,280 --> 00:38:01,040
Live offstage sound effects were often used,

630
00:38:01,140 --> 00:38:05,280
and music from a single piano to a full symphony orchestra

631
00:38:05,380 --> 00:38:07,280
was common.

632
00:38:07,380 --> 00:38:11,180
True synchronized sound, however, remained elusive.

633
00:38:14,200 --> 00:38:17,100
Spurred by recent improvements in his phonograph,

634
00:38:17,200 --> 00:38:21,600
Edison linked his sound machine with his movie equipment.

635
00:38:21,601 --> 00:38:24,900
The result was the kinetophone.

636
00:38:25,000 --> 00:38:28,880
The kinetophone was nothing more than a variable speed control

637
00:38:28,980 --> 00:38:31,580
on the phonograph that allowed the projectionist

638
00:38:31,680 --> 00:38:36,220
to adjust the sound to keep it in sync with the picture,

639
00:38:36,320 --> 00:38:38,980
hopefully.

640
00:38:39,080 --> 00:38:42,860
In February 1912, 50 kinetophones were introduced

641
00:38:42,960 --> 00:38:44,460
into the theaters.

642
00:38:44,560 --> 00:38:48,720
The New York Times reported gasps of astonishment.

643
00:38:48,721 --> 00:38:52,960
But soon, sound and picture began to drift apart.

644
00:38:53,060 --> 00:38:55,760
After the audience's expectations had been built up,

645
00:38:55,860 --> 00:39:01,400
to have the sound seriously out of sync with the performance

646
00:39:01,500 --> 00:39:06,580
on the screen led to uproar, people hooting, ridicule,

647
00:39:06,680 --> 00:39:09,580
critical newspaper articles making fun of this.

648
00:39:09,680 --> 00:39:13,680
And certainly, Edison wasn't pleased about ending up being

649
00:39:13,780 --> 00:39:16,340
an object of ridicule after all of the effort

650
00:39:16,341 --> 00:39:19,640
all of the effort that he put into this technology.

651
00:39:19,740 --> 00:39:22,920
So ended the longest single experiment

652
00:39:23,020 --> 00:39:26,280
in the history of Edison's laboratory.

653
00:39:26,380 --> 00:39:30,760
On March 30, 1918, the inventor sold his Bronx studio

654
00:39:30,860 --> 00:39:34,560
and all his motion picture interests.

655
00:39:34,660 --> 00:39:37,000
The end of his interest in the motion picture

656
00:39:37,100 --> 00:39:40,200
was more than the result of financial failure.

657
00:39:40,300 --> 00:39:45,300
It was also part of a pattern in his life.

658
00:39:45,301 --> 00:39:51,740
He lacked the capacity for sustained research in a single field.

659
00:39:51,741 --> 00:39:54,800
He starts in telegraphy, he gives up telegraphy,

660
00:39:54,801 --> 00:39:57,540
he moves to telephony, he gives up telephony,

661
00:39:57,541 --> 00:40:00,940
he moves to the phonograph, he goes to the electric lighting system,

662
00:40:00,941 --> 00:40:03,160
he comes back to motion pictures,

663
00:40:03,161 --> 00:40:08,060
which all of which have combinations and bits and pieces of each other,

664
00:40:08,061 --> 00:40:11,560
but which are not in fact the same system.

665
00:40:11,561 --> 00:40:14,460
While Edison drifted away from the movie business,

666
00:40:14,560 --> 00:40:17,960
the silent film was maturing into the central art form

667
00:40:18,060 --> 00:40:20,600
of the 20th century.

668
00:40:20,700 --> 00:40:24,600
D.W. Griffith would soon create the controversial classic

669
00:40:24,700 --> 00:40:27,300
Birth of a Nation.

670
00:40:27,400 --> 00:40:30,680
An English music hall comedian, Charlie Chaplin,

671
00:40:30,780 --> 00:40:33,040
would become first and perhaps the greatest

672
00:40:33,140 --> 00:40:36,380
international movie star.

673
00:40:36,480 --> 00:40:41,560
All this hardly 30 years after W.K.L. Dixon's pioneering

674
00:40:41,561 --> 00:40:45,860
experiments in the Black Mariah.

675
00:40:45,960 --> 00:40:50,300
But in the 1920s, no one knew Dixon's name.

676
00:40:50,400 --> 00:40:54,200
Thomas Edison was the inventor of the motion pictures,

677
00:40:54,300 --> 00:40:57,700
a living legend.

678
00:40:57,800 --> 00:41:02,120
He became the most widely known inventor of the age.

679
00:41:02,220 --> 00:41:06,020
In surveys of the top 10 figures in history,

680
00:41:06,120 --> 00:41:10,260
Edison often figured near the top, sometimes at the very top.

681
00:41:10,261 --> 00:41:14,440
very unusual for an inventor who were often seen as kind of odd folks.

682
00:41:15,500 --> 00:41:20,800
Yet here was Edison seeming to be both every man and this great genius at the same time.

683
00:41:22,080 --> 00:41:25,140
1927 was a landmark year.

684
00:41:25,640 --> 00:41:31,580
It was Edison's 80th birthday, but it was also the year that produced The Jazz Singer,

685
00:41:32,440 --> 00:41:34,640
the first successful talking picture.

686
00:41:36,620 --> 00:41:38,600
Movie technology had passed him by.

687
00:41:40,260 --> 00:41:44,140
But the old wizard could still charm newsreel reporters,

688
00:41:44,240 --> 00:41:46,260
poking fun at his lifelong hearing problem.

689
00:41:46,360 --> 00:41:48,600
What do you think of the sound pictures of today?

690
00:41:48,700 --> 00:41:52,180
What I think of the talking pictures.

691
00:41:52,280 --> 00:41:53,180
Yeah.

692
00:41:53,280 --> 00:41:56,980
Uh, well, I don't know.

693
00:41:57,080 --> 00:41:58,280
I never heard one.

694
00:42:02,340 --> 00:42:04,420
Well, I have a little trouble now and then,

695
00:42:04,520 --> 00:42:06,880
but that's because I'm getting old.

696
00:42:06,980 --> 00:42:08,820
But I've got a lot of ginger yet.

697
00:42:10,260 --> 00:42:16,160
Four months later, the wizard of Menlo Park was dead.

698
00:42:16,260 --> 00:42:20,600
The world mourned the man whose legend claimed the phonograph,

699
00:42:20,700 --> 00:42:25,800
the electric light, and the movies.

700
00:42:25,900 --> 00:42:29,140
Something so incredible.

701
00:42:29,240 --> 00:42:30,640
I trust my own.

702
00:42:30,740 --> 00:42:35,320
In 1935, four years later, suffering from hard times,

703
00:42:35,420 --> 00:42:39,020
another man died quietly in England, William Kennedy

704
00:42:39,021 --> 00:42:41,180
William Kennedy Laurie Dixon.

705
00:42:41,280 --> 00:42:45,800
To the end, Edison considered Dixon a double-crosser.

706
00:42:45,900 --> 00:42:48,260
Edison, and certainly the Edison legend,

707
00:42:48,360 --> 00:42:51,040
left no room for co-inventors.

708
00:42:51,140 --> 00:42:54,400
In fact, it was a true collaborative effort.

709
00:42:54,500 --> 00:43:00,840
Both of their roles were essential to achieve success.

710
00:43:00,940 --> 00:43:04,540
Despite Dixon's key role, other Edison scholars

711
00:43:04,640 --> 00:43:08,320
argue that Edison might have found another Dixon.

712
00:43:08,321 --> 00:43:12,960
But Dixon could never have found another Thomas Edison.

713
00:43:13,060 --> 00:43:19,260
This is Edison's death mask, never before seen on television.

714
00:43:19,360 --> 00:43:23,060
His expression serenely confident to the end,

715
00:43:23,160 --> 00:43:28,000
dogged and determined, ruthless when he needed to be,

716
00:43:28,100 --> 00:43:30,940
Thomas Edison may not have thought of all the ideas

717
00:43:31,040 --> 00:43:33,040
of his invention.

718
00:43:33,140 --> 00:43:35,680
But he was the man who got them made.

